        "lesson": "phy9-c1-s1-n9",
        "text": "分子运动论可以说明为什么物质可以处于气态、液态和固态。\n\n气体：在常见的稀薄气体近似中，原子或分子之间的平均距离比分子本身的大小大许多倍（图 $6$）。\n原书以 $1$ 个大气压下，容器体积为其中气体分子总体积的几万倍作数量级示例。\n这个比值还取决于温度、气体种类和怎样估计分子体积。\n\n图 6\n\n图 7\n\n稀薄气体比较容易被压缩，因为压缩主要减小了分子间的平均距离，而不是把分子本身压小（图 $7$）。\n这里采用分子大小远小于分子间距的近似；\n压缩到较高密度时，不能继续忽略分子大小和相互作用。\n\n在常见室温条件下，许多气体分子的速率为几百米每秒，但不同分子并不以同一速率运动。\n在稀薄气体的简单模型中，分子碰撞时像台球一样改变运动方向。\n\n对于处于热平衡的同一种气体，温度越高，分子的平均平动动能越大，典型速率也越大；\n这说的是统计关系，不是某个分子的速率决定整团气体的温度。\n\n在稀薄气体近似适用的条件下，分子间的吸引不足以把分子保持在紧挨着的状态。\n气体没有固定的形状和体积，会扩展到可用的容器空间。\n\n这不意味着任何条件下都能无限膨胀；\n容器和外界压强会限制它的体积，温度和压强改变时也可能发生凝结。\n\n大量分子对容器壁的多次碰撞，产生了气体的压强。\n\n液体：液体分子彼此靠得很近（图 $8$），运动受到邻近分子的约束，与稀薄气体中的运动不同。\n一种简单的近似把周围分子比作临时的“笼子”，分子在局部平衡位置附近振动。\n这不是固定不动的笼子，周围分子也在运动。\n\n有时，分子通过邻近分子的重新排列，离开原来的“笼子”，进入新邻居形成的“笼子”，可以形象地称为“跳跃”。\n原书给出的统计力学估计是：室温水分子在局部位置附近“定居”的平均时间为一万亿分之一秒，完成一次振动的时间为 $10^{-12}\\sim10^{-13}$ 秒。\n这些时间是该模型中的数量级估计，不能当作所有液体分子都遵守的固定周期。\n\n在这种近似通常适用的条件下，温度升高，分子“定居”的平均时间会缩短。\n原书把这种液体运动图像的建立归于苏联物理学家雅科夫·伊·弗兰克尔；\n\n这里保留这一历史归属，不把“首先”的说法作为独立核实的优先权结论。\n这个近似图像有助于定性理解液体的基本性质。\n\n图 8\n\n雅科夫·伊·弗兰克尔（$1894\\sim1952$）是苏联理论物理学家，曾研究物理学的多个领域。\n原书把液态近代理论的创立、铁磁理论基础的建立，以及铀核分裂的第一个定量理论归于他。\n\n原书还介绍了他关于大气电学和地球磁场起源的研究。\n这些表述保留原书的历史归属，不把“创造者”“第一个”等说法作为已经独立核实的排他性结论。\n\n液体分子彼此靠得很近。\n原书用分子形变解释液体受到挤压时的情形（图 $9$）；\n较稳妥的说明是，分子间距继续缩小时，短程排斥迅速增大。\n\n继续压缩通常需要很大的力，所以液体的压缩性较小，但不是完全不能压缩。\n不能把这种阻力只理解为分子本身发生字面意义的形变。\n\n液体能够流动，没有固定的形状。\n在“笼子”和“跳跃”的近似图像中，没有定向驱动的、各向同性的静止液体，分子向各个方向重新排列的机会相同，不产生整体的定向流动（图 $8$）。\n\n当有未被平衡的驱动作用，例如重力或运动固体的压力时，这个近似图像把流动解释为重新排列在驱动方向上略占优势（图 $10$）。\n持续的重新排列使液体能够流动，并在通常条件下取容器的形状。\n\n图 9\n\n图 10\n\n在弱驱动的简单近似中，单位时间内的跃迁次数主要取决于热运动，可以近似认为总次数没有明显变化，改变的主要是方向上的偏向。\n当作用时间比分子“定居”的平均时间长许多倍，液体就有机会通过反复重新排列发生流动；\n但时间足够长并不保证流动，还要看驱动是否被平衡、运动是否受阻。\n\n如果作用时间很短，短于有关的结构松弛时间，分子还来不及充分重新排列，液体可以表现出类似弹性固体的短时响应。\n这是与材料和观察时间有关的近似，不表示所有液体在任意短时作用下都与固体完全相同。\n\n固体：在通常的观察时间和较小外力下，固体中的粒子与邻近粒子的联系比较持久，主要在局部平衡位置附近振动。\n粒子有时也会改变位置，但在这些条件下通常比液体中的重新排列少得多，并不是绝对不能换位。\n因此，固体通常既有较稳定的体积，也有较稳定的形状。\n\n可以把液体比作成员不断换位的人群，把晶体比作成员在各自位置附近活动的整齐队伍；\n这个比喻只说明排列与运动的区别。\n对于原书这里讨论的周期晶体，粒子的平均位置具有长程周期排列，可以用空间点阵描述。\n\n非晶态固体没有这样的长程周期排列，不能把所有固体都看作晶体。\n食盐（图 $11$）、金刚石（图 $12$）和镁（图 $13$）是原书给出的晶体点阵例子；\n\n食盐中的粒子是离子，金刚石和镁中的粒子是原子。\n晶体内部的有序排列可以在适宜的生长条件下形成规则的几何外形（图 $14$），但不是每块晶体都具有完整的规则外形。\n\n图 11\n\n图 12\n\n图 13\n\n图 14\n\n分子运动论可以用比较直观的图像，定性说明物质的一些基本性质。\n要把实验测得的压强、温度等，与分子的性质、数目和运动速率联系成定量理论，就需要处理大量分子的统计行为。\n\n这样的理论比上面的定性图像复杂。\n后续内容只讨论气体的理论。\n\n首先介绍热力学。\n\n应当先说明表征宏观物体内部状态的量，学会测量这些量，并研究它们的变化。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c1-s1-n9",
        "text": "Molecular kinetic theory helps explain why matter can exist as a gas, a liquid, or a solid.\n\nGases: In the usual dilute-gas approximation, the average distance between atoms or molecules is much greater than the size of a molecule (Figure $6$).\nThe source gives an order-of-magnitude example: at $1$ atmosphere, the container volume is tens of thousands of times the total volume assigned to its gas molecules.\nThis ratio also depends on temperature, the gas, and how molecular volume is estimated.\n\nFigure 6\n\nFigure 7\n\nA dilute gas is relatively easy to compress because compression mainly reduces the average distance between molecules rather than making the molecules themselves smaller (Figure $7$).\nThis uses the approximation that molecular size is much smaller than molecular spacing;\nat higher densities, size and intermolecular interactions can no longer be neglected.\n\nUnder common room-temperature conditions, many gas molecules move at hundreds of meters per second, but individual molecules do not all have the same speed.\nIn a simple dilute-gas model, collisions change their directions, rather like collisions between billiard balls.\n\nFor the same gas in thermal equilibrium, a higher temperature means greater average translational kinetic energy and higher typical molecular speeds;\nthis is a statistical relationship, not a claim that one molecule's speed determines the temperature of the whole gas.\n\nWhere the dilute-gas approximation applies, intermolecular attraction is not enough to hold the molecules close together.\nA gas has no fixed shape or volume and expands into the available space in its container.\n\nThis does not mean unlimited expansion under every condition;\na container and external pressure constrain its volume, and changes in temperature and pressure can also lead to condensation.\n\nRepeated collisions of many molecules with the container walls produce gas pressure.\n\nLiquids: Molecules in a liquid are close together (Figure $8$), so neighboring molecules constrain their motion, unlike motion in a dilute gas.\nA simple approximation treats the neighbors as a temporary \"cage,\" with a molecule vibrating near a local equilibrium position.\n\nThe cage is not fixed: its neighboring molecules are also moving.\nSometimes a rearrangement lets a molecule leave its old \"cage\" and enter another formed by new neighbors, a motion pictured as \"hopping.\"\n\nThe source's statistical-mechanics estimate for water at room temperature gives an average residence time of one trillionth of a second and a time for one vibration of $10^{-12}\\sim10^{-13}$ seconds.\nThese are order-of-magnitude estimates within this model, not fixed periods obeyed by all liquid molecules.\n\nUnder conditions where this approximation usually applies, increasing temperature shortens the average residence time.\nThe source attributes this picture of liquid motion to the Soviet physicist Yakov I. Frenkel;\n\nits claim of priority is retained as historical attribution, not as an independently verified conclusion.\nThis approximate picture helps explain basic liquid properties qualitatively.\n\nFigure 8\n\nYakov I. Frenkel ($1894\\sim1952$) was a Soviet theoretical physicist who worked in several areas of physics.\nThe source credits him with creating modern liquid theory, laying foundations of ferromagnetic theory, and developing the first quantitative theory of uranium nuclear fission.\n\nThe source also describes his work on atmospheric electricity and the origin of Earth's magnetic field.\nThese statements preserve the textbook's historical attribution;\nlabels such as \"creator\" and \"first\" are not presented as independently verified, exclusive historical conclusions.\n\nLiquid molecules are close together.\nThe source explains compression in terms of molecular deformation (Figure $9$);\n\na more careful explanation is that short-range repulsion increases sharply as molecular spacing is reduced further.\nFurther compression usually requires a large force, so liquids have low, but not zero, compressibility.\nThis resistance should not be understood only as literal deformation of the molecules themselves.\n\nA liquid can flow and has no fixed shape.\nIn the approximate cage-and-hopping picture, an isotropic liquid at rest with no directional driving has equal opportunities for rearrangements in all directions, with no overall directed flow (Figure $8$).\n\nWith an unbalanced driving influence, such as gravity or pressure from a moving solid, this picture describes flow as a slight bias of rearrangements in the driving direction (Figure $10$).\nContinuing rearrangements allow the liquid to flow and, under usual conditions, take the shape of its container.\n\nFigure 9\n\nFigure 10\n\nIn a simple weak-driving approximation, thermal motion mainly sets the number of hops per unit time, so the total rate can be treated as approximately unchanged while the main change is a directional bias.\nWhen the action lasts much longer than the average residence time, repeated rearrangements have time to support flow;\n\na long duration alone does not guarantee flow, because the driving may be balanced or the motion constrained.\nFor action on a very short timescale, shorter than the relevant structural relaxation time, molecules cannot rearrange fully, and a liquid can show a short-time response like an elastic solid.\nThis is an approximation dependent on the material and observation time, not a claim that every liquid becomes identical to a solid under any brief action.\n\nSolids: Over usual observation times and under small external forces, particles in a solid keep relatively persistent connections with their neighbors and mainly vibrate near local equilibrium positions.\nParticles can sometimes change position, but under these conditions rearrangement is usually much less frequent than in a liquid, rather than absolutely impossible.\nA solid therefore usually retains a relatively stable volume and shape.\n\nA liquid can be compared to a crowd whose members keep changing places, and a crystal to an orderly group whose members move around their assigned positions;\nthis analogy only illustrates differences in arrangement and motion.\n\nIn the periodic crystals discussed here, particles' average positions have a long-range periodic arrangement that can be described by a spatial lattice.\nAmorphous solids lack this long-range periodic arrangement, so not all solids should be treated as crystals.\n\nTable salt (Figure $11$), diamond (Figure $12$), and magnesium (Figure $13$) are the source's examples of crystal lattices;\nthe particles are ions in salt and atoms in diamond and magnesium.\nThe ordered internal arrangement can produce regular geometric faces under suitable growth conditions (Figure $14$), but not every piece of a crystal has a complete regular outer shape.\n\nFigure 11\n\nFigure 12\n\nFigure 13\n\nFigure 14\n\nMolecular kinetic theory offers fairly intuitive pictures that explain some basic properties of matter qualitatively.\nA quantitative theory relating measured pressure and temperature to molecular properties, numbers, and speeds must handle the statistical behavior of many molecules.\n\nSuch a theory is more complex than the qualitative pictures above.\nThe following material considers only gas theory.\n\nWe begin with thermodynamics.\n\nFirst, we need to describe the quantities that characterize a macroscopic body's internal state, learn how to measure them, and study how they change."
      }
